Overview {#mainpage}
========

WiiLand is an open-source device driver for Nintendo Wii / Wii U remotes. It
contains tools and libraries which work together with the official hid-wiimote
kernel driver, available since linux-3.1.

If you want to use a Nintendo Wii Remote or any compatible device (including the
Nintendo Wii Balance Board, Nintendo Wii U Pro Controller, and more) on your
linux system, the WiiLand software stack provides everything you need.

This distribution is hosted at:
  https://github.com/PhilosophiMoonbeam/wiiland
Use this website to contact the maintainers or developers or to file bug
reports.

Install
=======

To install the libxwiimote.so library and the related tools, use:
	$ ./configure [--enable-debug] [--prefix=/usr]
	$ make
	$ make install
If "configure" is not available, use:
	$ ./autogen.sh [<configure-flags>]

Dependencies:
	- libudev: Used for device enumeration
	- ncurses: Used for UI of xwiishow
	- Linux uinput: Used by wiilandd to expose Wayland-native virtual
		input devices
	- Qt 6 Widgets: Optional; used only when building wiiland-config with
		--enable-qt-ui

This software packages contains:
	libxwiimote.so: A userspace library which helps accessing connected Wii
		Remotes in the system. It can be used by applications to use Wii
		Remotes as input. You can also use the direct kernel interface,
		though.
	xwiishow: A test application which lists all connected Wii Remotes. If a
		Wii Remote is passed as argument, it continuously reads input
		from the device and prints it to the screen.
	wiilandd: A Wayland-native bridge which reads libxwiimote events
		and creates Linux uinput virtual controllers. Wayland compositors,
		SDL, Wine/Proton, and native games can consume these devices
		through the normal evdev/libinput stack without XWayland.
	wiiland-config: Optional Qt 6 desktop frontend for configuring
		wiilandd profiles, running diagnostics, and collecting validation
		traces. Build with --enable-qt-ui when Qt6Widgets is installed.
	50-xorg-fix-wiiland.conf: Legacy Xorg blacklist example kept in the
		source tree for distributions that still maintain Xorg sessions.
		The Wayland install path does not install it by default.
	manpages: Several manpages are provided. One overview page and several
		other pages for each tool and feature. They are installed by
		the autotools scripts automatically alongside the library and
		applications.

The following tools are available but not installed into the system:
	xwiidump: A test application which reads the EEPROM memory of a
		connected Wii Remote and prints it to stdout. This requires
		debugfs support in the kernel and the hid-wiimote kernel module.

Following software is not part of this package:
	hid-wiimote.ko: The wiimote kernel module is available in the official
		linux kernel sources and should already be installed in your
		system if you run linux-3.1 or newer.
	wiimote.so: The BlueZ bluetoothd wiimote plugin is part of the upstream
		BlueZ package and available since bluez-4.96. It should be
		already installed on your system.

Usage
=====

Please see the website for help:
  https://github.com/PhilosophiMoonbeam/wiiland

For Wayland game sessions, run:
	$ wiilandd --list
	$ wiilandd

For desktop pointer/keyboard control, run:
	$ wiilandd --profile desktop

For one process that exposes both gamepad and desktop controls, run:
	$ wiilandd --profile both

To tune desktop pointer speed and IR gain persistently:
	$ mkdir -p ~/.config/wiiland
	$ printf 'profile=desktop\npointer-speed=24\nir-speed=10\n' > ~/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf

For Steam-style or non-Steam motion aiming, keep the normal gamepad profile and
add a WiiLand aim output. `right-stick` feeds camera aim through ABS_RX/ABS_RY;
`mouse` feeds a virtual relative pointer for games that accept mouse aim:
	$ printf 'profile=gamepad\naim-mode=right-stick\naim-source=auto\naim-activation=b\n' > ~/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf

For Wii-style flat-surface motion calibration, place the Wii Remote face down
with buttons against a stable table and paste the generated offsets into the
same config:
	$ wiilandd --device 1 --calibrate-aim >> ~/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf


System-wide defaults can use `/etc/wiiland/wiilandd.conf`; user config files
override system defaults.

Per-device rules can override that default by sysfs path substring:
	$ printf 'profile=gamepad\ndevice.bluetooth.profile=desktop\n' > ~/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf

For the optional Qt 6 frontend, configure with `--enable-qt-ui` and run:
	$ wiiland-config

For hardware-free diagnostics in packaging or CI, run:
	$ wiilandd --check-config --config res/wiilandd.conf
	$ wiilandd --dump-config --config res/wiilandd.conf
	$ wiilandd --doctor --config res/wiilandd.conf
	$ wiilandd --axis-map
	$ wiilandd --validation-checklist
	$ wiilandd --self-test
	$ wiilandd --dry-run --trace-events --verbose

`wiilandd --help` and `wiilandd --list` intentionally bypass configuration so
diagnostics still work when a local config file is broken.

The Wayland bridge needs read access to hid-wiimote `/dev/input/event*` nodes and
write access to `/dev/uinput`. See ./doc/WIILAND for the migration plan, backend
rationale, the sample config in ./res/wiilandd.conf, the installable udev access
rule in ./res/70-wiiland-uinput.rules, and the example user service in
./res/wiilandd.service.in. The service runs `wiilandd --check-config` before
starting so broken configuration fails fast. The installed unit is generated with
the configured `bindir`, so `/usr` and `/usr/local` prefix builds use the
matching `wiilandd` binary path.

To run the packaged user service in graphical sessions:
	$ systemctl --user daemon-reload
	$ systemctl --user enable wiilandd.service
	$ systemctl --user start wiilandd.service

`make install` installs the uinput udev rule, systemd user service, and sample
configuration under the selected prefix.

Documentation
=============

./doc/WIILAND: Wayland migration plan, validation matrix, and packaging notes
./doc/wiiland.7, ./doc/libxwiimote.7, ./doc/xwiishow.1, ./doc/wiilandd.1:
	Installed manpages
./doc/DEVICES: Enumeration of all Nintendo Wii / Wii U related devices
./doc/DEV_*: Device communication/protocol descriptions

Development
===========

Please see ./DEV for development information.

Run `tests/wiilandd-smoke.sh` to compile the hardware-free `wiilandd` smoke
binary, exercise deterministic self-tests, validate the sample config, and
render the daemon manpage when `groff` is installed.

Run `tools/wiilandd-hardware-report.sh` on a Wayland session with real hardware
attached to collect the required validation metadata and dry-run event traces.

Copying
=======

Please see ./COPYING for more information.

Compatibility
=============

Two other commonly used Wii-Remote libraries are cwiid and wiiuse. Both provide
a full user-space driver and were used heavily in the past. We do not provide
API compatibility to them as both APIs are very restricted:

	- cwiid: This API is highly asynchronous with threads. It does not allow
	  a single-threaded application to use it. This makes it very hard to
	  use and we decided not to provide a compatibility layer as we do not
	  want to support such library-API designs.
	- wiiuse: This API is tightly bound to the idea that the application is
	  supposed to discover and connect the Bluetooth devices. It uses a
	  static array for all used Wii Remotes and requires callbacks for
	  notification. Same as cwiid, we do not provide a compatibility layer
	  as the API is very different from our API.

We designed our API to have as few restrictions as possible. We do not force an
application to use a special programming technique. Instead, an application can
use our API to support event-based, callback-based or thread-based event-loops
or any other kind that it wants. We achieve this by keeping the user-space API
very similar to the kernel-API so the application can integrate this into any
infrastructure it wants.

